From: Fathom <fathom@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] init: a tenth of a second, not one, after SIGTERM and before the reboot call

At halt, power-off and reboot, init runs the shutdown actions (rcK, then
"umount -a -r"), sends SIGTERM to everything, sleeps a second, sends SIGKILL,
and sleeps a second more before the reboot() call, "for the last message to
reach the serial console". On the H2 the stop scripts have by then stopped
everything that has anything to save (the player, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, SSH) and
unmounted the card (S10sdcard), so the two seconds are pure waiting with the
screen saying "Powering off". A tenth of a second each: enough for what's
left (getty, udhcpd, shells) to take the signal, and for the console.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Fathom's stop scripts make it safe]

--- a/init/init.c
+++ b/init/init.c
@@ -735,8 +735,9 @@
 {
 	pid_t pid;
 
-	/* Allow time for last message to reach serial console, etc */
-	sleep1();
+	/* Allow time for last message to reach serial console, etc.
+	 * Fathom: a tenth of a second, not one (see below) */
+	msleep(100);
 
 	/* We have to fork here, since the kernel calls do_exit(EXIT_SUCCESS)
 	 * in linux/kernel/sys.c, which can cause the machine to panic when
@@ -768,7 +769,10 @@
 	kill(-1, SIGTERM);
 	message(L_CONSOLE, "Sent SIG%s to all processes", "TERM");
 	sync();
-	sleep1();
+	/* Fathom: a tenth of a second, not one. The stop scripts have already
+	 * stopped everything that has anything to save and unmounted the card
+	 * (S90fathom ... S10sdcard), so nothing left needs a second to end. */
+	msleep(100);
 
 	kill(-1, SIGKILL);
 	message(L_CONSOLE, "Sent SIG%s to all processes", "KILL");
